  "prompt_text": "This figure contrasts large-scale climate setups linked to enhanced versus reduced rainfall over East Africa. In the upper map, wetter conditions are associated with a combination of El Niño in the Pacific, a positive Indian Ocean Dipole, and an active MJO over the western Indian Ocean. These patterns appear to warm the western Indian Ocean and strengthen rising motion there. Large blue arrows indicate an Indian Ocean Walker circulation with ascent over East Africa and nearby ocean regions, while labels also suggest westerly winds from the Congo and relatively cooler sea-surface temperatures off southwestern Africa helping funnel moisture inland. Together, the circulation and moisture transport favor stronger rainfall over the East African region highlighted in green.\n\nIn the lower map, the opposite ocean state is shown for reduced rainfall: La Niña in the Pacific and a negative IOD in the Indian Ocean. The Walker circulation is reversed, with sinking motion over East Africa and stronger ascent farther east near Indonesia. This shift implies less moisture convergence and weaker uplift over East Africa, suppressing rainfall. Overall, the figure shows how Pacific and Indian Ocean variability jointly reorganize tropical circulation and determine whether East Africa becomes unusually wet or dry.",
